# Line endings in the repository are LF, always. What a checkout gets mirrors .editorconfig: CRLF for
# the Windows projects' own file types, LF for everything else. Without this file the result depended
# on each machine's core.autocrlf, so the same commit checked out differently on two laptops and a
# formatter on one of them rewrote every line of a file it touched.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.cs text eol=crlf
*.csproj text eol=crlf
*.props text eol=crlf
*.targets text eol=crlf
*.axaml text eol=crlf
*.xaml text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.sln text eol=crlf
# The solution here is Flint.slnx, which Visual Studio rewrites with CRLF on any solution edit.
*.slnx text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell entry points break outright when a carriage return reaches the interpreter.
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Declared rather than detected. Git guesses "binary" from a NUL byte, and a golden vector that
# happens to contain none would be treated as text and have its line endings converted, which
# silently changes the wire bytes three languages are asserting against.
*.bin binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.jar binary
*.apk binary
*.aab binary
*.jks binary
*.keystore binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.nupkg binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary

# Generated and lock files are reviewed by what they do, not line by line.
Cargo.lock linguist-generated=true
